    "text": "The dispatch office was a bunker of light and silence, buried in the night. The only sounds were the low, constant hum of the monitors and the occasional crackle of a radio. Elias had been a dispatcher for thirty years, and the rhythm of the job was in his bones: the green blips on the screen, the disembodied voices of engineers, the immutable logic of the timetable.\n\nHis shift was a quiet one, a slow ballet of freight trains and the last few passenger services. At 2:17 a.m., a signal failure near the junction required him to fall back on the old ways. He pulled a thick, dog-eared binder from the shelf\u2014the 1998 edition of the Northern Line timetable. It was obsolete, a relic, but it held the old block schedules, the ones he\u2019d memorized as a young man.\n\nHe flipped it open, the paper smelling of dust and old glue. As he traced a line with his finger, a small, folded piece of paper, yellowed with age, slipped from between the pages and fluttered onto his desk.\n\nIt was a note, written in a neat, looping cursive he didn't recognize.\n\n*Elias,*\n\n*The 11:55 to Crewe is running seven minutes late. Don't let it meet the 12:10 freight at the Haltwhistle crossing. Give it the long loop. It's worth the wait.*\n\n*Be safe.*\n\nThere was no signature.\n\nElias stared at the note. His name, in a script he hadn't seen in decades",
